SQL RIGHT JOIN

The RIGHT JOIN returns all rows from the right table (`table2`) and only the matched rows from the left table (`table1`).
If there is no match in the left table, the columns from the left table will be `NULL`.

The RIGHT JOIN and RIGHT OUTER JOIN keywords are equal. The `OUTER` keyword is optional.

RIGHT JOIN Syntax
SELECT column_name(s)
FROM table1
RIGHT JOIN table2
ON table1.column_name = table2.column_name;

- `table1` is the left table.
- `table2` is the right table.
- The `ON` clause defines the matching condition.
- The result keeps every row from the right table, even when there is no matching row on the left.

Simple explanation:
Think of the right table as your main list and the left table as optional details. RIGHT JOIN keeps all items from the main list on the right, and adds data from the left only when there is a match.

