RIGHT JOIN Example
This query returns all employees and any orders they might have handled:

Example:
SELECT Orders.OrderID, Employees.LastName, Employees.FirstName
FROM Orders
RIGHT JOIN Employees ON Orders.EmployeeID = Employees.EmployeeID
ORDER BY Orders.OrderID;

What this query does:
- returns every row from `Employees` (the right table)
- includes matching rows from `Orders`
- shows `NULL` in the `Orders.OrderID` column when an employee has no matching order

Important note:
`RIGHT JOIN` returns all rows from the right table even when there are no matching rows in the left table.

Real-world meaning:
If you want to list all employees and also show their orders if any exist, RIGHT JOIN keeps every employee in the result and adds order data when available.

When to use RIGHT JOIN:
- When you want every row from the right table.
- When the right table contains the primary data you need.
- When the left table contains optional related data.

Comparison to LEFT JOIN:
- `LEFT JOIN` returns all rows from the left table.
- `RIGHT JOIN` returns all rows from the right table.
- If the same query is written with the tables swapped, LEFT JOIN and RIGHT JOIN can return equivalent results.

Example with table aliases:
SELECT o.OrderID, e.LastName, e.FirstName
FROM Orders AS o
RIGHT JOIN Employees AS e ON o.EmployeeID = e.EmployeeID;

This query does the same work but uses shorter table names for readability.
